namespace pdg { /**

\class ISerializable
\brief Interface for an object which can be serialized.
\ingroup Serialization

Declare a JavaScript class extending `pdg.ISerializable` and implement these
four methods:

- `getSerializedSize(serializer)` returns the byte count for the object's data.
  Use the serializer's `sizeof_*()` methods to match its encoding.
- `serialize(serializer)` writes the object's fields using `serialize_*()`.
- `deserialize(deserializer)` reads them using `deserialize_*()`, in the same
  order and with the same types used when writing.
- `getMyClassTag()` returns a stable, unique unsigned 32-bit tag identifying the
  class. Zero is invalid; `0xFFFFFF00` through `0xFFFFFFFF` are reserved by PDG.

The native constructor takes four callbacks in that order. Pass forwarding
functions to `super()` so native serialization calls your class methods with the
correct receiver. Initialize instance fields after `super()` returns.

Register the class with `pdg.registerSerializableClass()` before deserializing
its objects. The registered constructor must support being called without
arguments, so provide defaults for any constructor parameters.

Example
-------

\code{.js}
class MySerializableClass extends pdg.ISerializable {
    constructor(one = 15, two = 99) {
        super(
            function(serializer) { return this.getSerializedSize(serializer); },
            function(serializer) { this.serialize(serializer); },
            function(deserializer) { this.deserialize(deserializer); },
            function() { return this.getMyClassTag(); }
        );
        this.one = one;
        this.two = two;
    }

    getSerializedSize(serializer) {
        return serializer.sizeof_1u(this.one) + serializer.sizeof_2u(this.two);
    }

    serialize(serializer) {
        serializer.serialize_1u(this.one);
        serializer.serialize_2u(this.two);
    }

    deserialize(deserializer) {
        this.one = deserializer.deserialize_1u();
        this.two = deserializer.deserialize_2u();
    }

    getMyClassTag() {
        return 0x4068; // reserve a unique tag for this class in your application
    }
}

pdg.registerSerializableClass(MySerializableClass);
const original = new MySerializableClass();

const serializer = new pdg.Serializer();
serializer.serialize_obj(original);
serializer.serialize_obj(original); // writes a reference to the same object

const deserializer = new pdg.Deserializer();
deserializer.setDataPtr(serializer.getDataPtr());
const first = deserializer.deserialize_obj();
const second = deserializer.deserialize_obj();

console.assert(first instanceof MySerializableClass);
console.assert(first.one === 15 && first.two === 99);
console.assert(second === first); // shared references preserve object identity
\endcode

Objects can serialize other objects with `serialize_obj()`. The serializer
tracks objects within the stream, so repeated references and parent/child cycles
do not require duplicate object data. Use `sizeof_obj()` when calculating space
for nested serialized objects.

\sa Serializer
\sa Deserializer
\sa pdg.registerSerializableClass
*/ }
